<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP, permitted deliberately.

  From Android 9 the platform blocks `http://` outright unless an application says
  otherwise, and it blocks it *silently* from the caller's point of view: OkHttp
  throws before a packet leaves the device, so a working server, correct
  credentials and a live subscription all arrive at the user as "we could not
  reach your provider". That is exactly the failure this file removes.

  It is not a shortcut. IPTV panels are overwhelmingly served over plain HTTP —
  the Xtream API, the playlist and the streams themselves — and an IPTV player
  that refuses cleartext is an IPTV player that works with almost no provider.
  The user types their own server address; refusing to connect to it is not a
  security decision made on their behalf, it is the product not working.

  What is *not* given up: `trust-anchors` stays at the system store, so every
  `https://` provider is still verified normally. There is no user-CA trust here
  and no per-domain exemption to a weaker check — the only thing relaxed is the
  scheme, and only because the provider chose it.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
